{"vid-math":{"text":"Here is a measure cut into ten parts. Eight are shaded. That's eight-tenths. Now group the parts in twos. Every two small parts make one bigger part. Count again. Four parts are shaded, and there are five in all. Eight divided by two is four. Ten divided by two is five. Eight-tenths equals four-fifths. Line up the two strips. The shaded parts are the same length. Here is sixteen-twentieths. Sofia divides both numbers by two, and gets eight-tenths. She divides by two again, and gets four-fifths. Small steps. Alex sees that four divides both numbers. Sixteen divided by four is four. Twenty divided by four is five. Four-fifths, in one big step. Both ways land in the same place. Can we divide four and five by the same number? Only by one. So four-fifths is in simplest form. It has the fewest, biggest parts. Watch out! Divide both numbers by the same number. If you divide only one number, or take away, the shaded part changes size.","words":[{"text":"Here","type":"word","start":0,"end":0.24},{"text":"is","type":"word","start":0.32,"end":0.4},{"text":"a","type":"word","start":0.48,"end":0.56},{"text":"measure","type":"word","start":0.76,"end":0.96},{"text":"cut","type":"word","start":1.04,"end":1.28},{"text":"into","type":"word","start":1.36,"end":1.52},{"text":"ten","type":"word","start":1.68,"end":1.92},{"text":"parts.","type":"word","start":2,"end":2.56},{"text":"Eight","type":"word","start":3.04,"end":3.28},{"text":"are","type":"word","start":3.44,"end":3.52},{"text":"shaded.","type":"word","start":3.6,"end":4.28},{"text":"That's","type":"word","start":4.48,"end":4.72},{"text":"eight-tenths.","type":"word","start":4.88,"end":5.76},{"text":"Now","type":"word","start":6.4,"end":6.64},{"text":"group","type":"word","start":6.72,"end":6.88},{"text":"the","type":"word","start":6.96,"end":7.04},{"text":"parts","type":"word","start":7.12,"end":7.36},{"text":"in","type":"word","start":7.44,"end":7.6},{"text":"twos.","type":"word","start":7.68,"end":8.32},{